WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.919
If you look closely at this pack of
riders in the Tour de France, you'll see

00:00:07.919 --> 00:00:12.960
some of the best cyclists in the world.
They're here in the back.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:16.860
They're keeping up but also resting while their teammates in the front do most of the work.

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:20.480
It's a technique called drafting
and it's what helps them survive the

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:24.210
three-week race around France. But
drafting only really makes a difference

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:29.310
on these parts of the route: the long
flat and hilly stages. Eventually these

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:33.239
riders reach stages where they won't be
able to rely on others. They have to rely

00:00:33.239 --> 00:00:37.800
on their own strength and endurance to
win the race. That happens here on the

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:41.700
climbs. They're the most brutal and
exciting parts of the race and they're

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:46.610
what makes the Tour de France the most
famous bike ride in the world...

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.540
The Tour de France began as a desperate
ploy to sell more copies of the sports

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:06.730
newspaper, L'Auto. The paper was
struggling, so 1903 its editor, Henri Desgranges

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:11.530
organized a 19 day, 2,400
kilometer bike race around the country.

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:16.720
It went on to become such a success that
L'Auto made it an annual event. Year after

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:20.470
year they added new routes to make the
tour more challenging and also more

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:25.720
interesting for people to follow. By 1908,
L'Auto's sales had more than doubled. Then

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:30.700
in 1910, one of Desgranges' writers,
Alphonse Steines, suggested adding a new

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:36.100
twist to the route: the Tourmalet. It was a
brutal 19 kilometer uphill climb

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:41.380
ascending 1400 meters to the
summit. To see if this was even possible

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:45.190
Steines jumped in his car to make the
climb. He drove up, but his car got stuck

00:01:45.190 --> 00:01:48.430
in the snow at the top,
he suffered from hypothermia and nearly

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:53.640
died. But nonetheless he sent a telegram
saying: Tourmalet crossed. Very good road.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:58.990
perfectly passable. So the Tourmalet made its debut in the 1910 Tour. French

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:02.979
cyclist Octave Lapize was the first to
make the climb successfully. But he had

00:02:02.979 --> 00:02:06.580
to walk up some parts and famously
called officials assassins when he

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:10.539
reached the summit. But he went on to win the whole tour and his statue was placed

00:02:10.539 --> 00:02:15.760
at the top of the Tourmalet. Since then,
climbs became a staple of the Tour de

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:23.980
France. This year the route is made up of
21 stages over 23 days. It features 30

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:28.520
major climbs; seven of them are in the
tour's most difficult category.

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:33.569
Including the Tourmalet, which is part of
the event for the 86th time. More than

00:02:33.569 --> 00:02:38.160
any other climb in the Tour's history.
These climbs are where the tour is

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:43.709
ultimately won or lost. These are the
long, flat, and hilly stages that are

00:02:43.709 --> 00:02:47.819
usually about 200 kilometers long.
Here cyclists ride together in a

00:02:47.819 --> 00:02:52.500
formation, called a peloton. It allows
cyclists to save energy by sitting

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:57.480
behind another rider or drafting. At high
speeds, riders use most of their energy

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:01.709
to pedal against wind resistance. But
when a rider stays close behind another,

00:03:01.709 --> 00:03:05.549
they're sheltered from much of that. So,
pedaling becomes much easier and they

00:03:05.549 --> 00:03:09.780
can keep up with the front riders. The
way to measure this is to look at how

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:14.280
much power a cyclist generates. Here at
the front of the peloton, a Tour de

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:19.140
France rider will generate at least 300
watts of power. I jumped on a bike to see

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:23.940
what that feels like and just two
kilometers holding 300 watts was really

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:29.970
really hard. By comparison when a rider
is behind the lead in the peloton, they

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:35.400
only need to generate about 240 watts to
move at the same speed. Holding 240 watts

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:39.810
for two kilometers felt remarkably
easier. So even though these two might

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:44.549
finish a 200 kilometer flat stage at the
same time, one is going to be way less

00:03:44.549 --> 00:03:48.600
tired than the other. That's why you see
some of the tour's best riders here in

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:52.290
the back. They're drafting up their
teammates whose job it is to do the hard

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:56.670
work now so that the team's best cyclist
is rested for the most difficult part;

00:03:56.670 --> 00:04:01.080
the mountains. Where they'll have to be
on their own.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:06.450
When the peloton starts pedaling uphill,
it slows down. At this stage the race is

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:10.380
less about fighting wind resistance and
more about gravity, which affects all

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:14.850
riders the same way in the peloton. So,
now every rider in the front and the

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:20.549
back needs to generate an extraordinary
amount of power to keep up pace. In 2010

00:04:20.549 --> 00:04:24.450
for instance, Danish cyclist Chris Anker
Sorensen made it to the front of the

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:28.170
pack on the Tourmalet climb. "Look at the
face on Chris Anker Sorenesen. Dishing out the

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:31.800
pain at the front end. As the lead, he
dictated the pace of the whole group.

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:36.810
This chart shows his power output on the
final climb. He averaged 415 watts for

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:42.030
more than 11 minutes. "...face of Chris Anker Sorensen now, really dishing out the pain.

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:47.640
And around here he peaked at an
incredible 590 watts. "magnificent riding by

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:51.750
Chris Anker Sorenson, but how long can he keep this up? "Now look at American cyclist

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:56.190
Chris Horner. Even though he was several
positions behind Sorenson, his power

00:04:56.190 --> 00:05:01.320
output was almost the same. The faster
Sorenson climbed, the harder it was for

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:05.370
the rest of the peloton to keep up. And
so the formation started to break up as

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:11.580
weaker riders fell behind. This is the
moment in the race when the tour's best

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:15.920
riders switch from drafting to relying
on their own strength to get ahead.

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:21.000
Andy Schleck has finally attacked and Alberto Contador has gone with him.

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:25.710
The race is breaking up behind them like two of the best riders here:

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:29.700
Luxembourgian Andy Schleck and Spaniard Alberto Contador, who were coasting in the back

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:33.210
of the peloton during the long flat
stages. But here they are halfway up the

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:38.130
Tourmalet, breaking away for the win. each likely generating well over 400 watts

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:42.720
for the final eight kilometers. Contador
in the yellow jersey, was the overall

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:47.760
Tour de France leader but only by eight
seconds. In second place was Schleck, who

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:50.400
would try and lose him on this climb.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:52.400
"Andy Schleck is riding like a man possessed"

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:54.210
It was neck-and-neck to the very top...

00:05:54.210 --> 00:06:00.320
It's Schleck on the right. Contador! Schleck wins! Contador takes second!

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:04.500
Schleck edged Contador by a hair at the top of the Tourmalet to win the stage. But since

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:07.710
he didn't lose him,
Contador kept his overall lead and went

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:11.880
on to win the Tour de France. This kind
of drama is only possible in the

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:18.690
mountains and this year's route makes
climbs particularly important.

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:22.620
This year's Tour is being called the highest in history because of how many climbs

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:27.990
there are over the course of three weeks.
Just one day features seven climbs. Even

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:31.650
after more than two weeks in the race,
riders climb to 2,770 meters

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:36.960
above sea level where the thin air makes climbing even harder.

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:39.200
That's what makes the Tour
de France the most grueling and

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:43.380
prestigious race in this sport. The
winner isn't simply the strongest rider

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:48.389
but the one who endures the most pain
and ultimately has what it takes to

00:06:48.389 --> 00:06:51.349
conquer the mountains.

